> Or did I get the role wrong and should still be fighting most of the 
> time in an ordinary way with my quaterstaff?  But this (or my character) 
> seems to be pretty weak, so this won't let me survive for long, I think...

This is (one of) the canonical early game wizard strategies: 
First, run away a lot. Use your spells to get you out of tight spots, so
be sure to have some mana reserves for emergencies. If you find armor with
high AC effects, consider wearing it even if it is metallic and drives your
spell success rates down (mithril is an example). Many players abandon
spells alltogether in the early game in favor of AC. If you chose
otherwise, still wear at least studded leather and a dwarven iron helm,
combined with a small shield that you can take off if you need to cast
higher level spells.

One good weapon type for wizards is dagger. They can get to expert in it,
and will receive Magicbane as a first gift, which is a very useful weapon.
Wield an elven dagger and collect normal ones for throwing. Build up a good
throwing stack and make ranged combat with daggers and spells your
priority. Advance dagger skill instead of quarterstaff.

You will notice that a wizard profits immensely from experience levels.
They need it for the skill slots in the magical schools, for the mana
points, and for the spell damage (it factors into the effect of most attack
spells). By the time you reach the quest, your character will have
gradually evolved from a cautious fighter to a spellcaster.

